<h2>How the matter reached an ex parte stage</h2>
<p>The petitioner invoked Section 11(6) after the appointment mechanism failed in the usual way — and the respondent then declined even to receive the petition. The Court recorded that service had been refused and deemed it sufficient; when the matter was called and no appearance was entered, it proceeded ex parte. The arbitration agreement itself was not in dispute on the record. That combination — deemed service, non-appearance, undisputed clause — left nothing for the referral court to withhold.</p>

<h2>The narrow gate of Section 11</h2>
<p>The order situates itself squarely in the modern referral jurisprudence. Section 11(6A) confines the court to the existence of the arbitration agreement, and <em>Krish Spinning</em> — cited in the order — confirms that the examination is prima facie only, with everything else reserved to the tribunal&rsquo;s own competence under Section 16. The practical consequence is visible here: the Court did not inquire into the underlying dispute between the tent services contractor and the government, did not test limitation, and did not weigh defences. It found a prima facie agreement, made the appointment, and moved every substantive question downstream.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Pre-2015 referral practice</h3>
    <p>Referral courts could travel into arbitrability, limitation and even merits, making Section 11 a preliminary trial and an invitation to delay.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>The current discipline</h3>
    <p>Prima facie existence of the agreement is the sole question; the tribunal decides the rest under Section 16, with DIAC&rsquo;s institutional framework supplying rules and fees.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The appointment&rsquo;s institutional frame</h2>
<p>Three features of the operative directions deserve note. The arbitrator must enter reference within a fixed period and make disclosures under Section 12(2) — the independence and impartiality statement that undergirds the validity of the appointment. The arbitration proceeds under the Delhi International Arbitration Centre&rsquo;s rules and fee schedule, giving both sides a published procedural framework and predictable costs. And all pleas are preserved: the respondent that stayed away arrives before the tribunal with its jurisdictional and substantive arsenal intact, subject only to the tribunal&rsquo;s timelines.</p>

<h2>Lessons on both sides of the aisle</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For claimants:</strong> document the service trail meticulously — the dispatch, the refusal endorsement, the tracking record. Deemed service is only as strong as its proof.</p>
  <p><strong>For respondents, especially public bodies:</strong> refusing service buys nothing. The appointment happens anyway; the only casualty is participation in constituting the tribunal. The economical course is to appear, reserve objections, and fight where the statute locates the fight — before the arbitrator.</p>
  <p><strong>For both:</strong> an undisputed clause ends the referral inquiry. Energy spent resisting Section 11 petitions on grounds the tribunal will decide is energy, and fee, wasted.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information and is not advice on any particular matter.</div>
